<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Agent memory · Agentic Bible</title><meta name="description" content="How an agent stores and retrieves information across steps or sessions, and why persistence, relevance, and truth are separate questions."><meta property="og:title" content="Agent memory · Agentic Bible"><meta property="og:description" content="How an agent stores and retrieves information across steps or sessions, and why persistence, relevance, and truth are separate questions."><meta property="og:type" content="article"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Agentic Bible"><meta property="og:url" content="https://agenticbible.si/articles/agent-memory/"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary"><meta name="twitter:title" content="Agent memory · Agentic Bible"><meta name="twitter:description" content="How an agent stores and retrieves information across steps or sessions, and why persistence, relevance, and truth are separate questions."><meta name="robots" content="index, follow"><meta name="color-scheme" content="light"><link rel="canonical" href="https://agenticbible.si/articles/agent-memory/"><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"TechArticle","headline":"Agent memory","description":"How an agent stores and retrieves information across steps or sessions, and why persistence, relevance, and truth are separate questions.","articleSection":"Capabilities","inLanguage":"en","url":"https://agenticbible.si/articles/agent-memory/","mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://agenticbible.si/articles/agent-memory/"},"citation":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03442v2","https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08560v2","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/concepts/memory"]}</script><link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/_astro/Base.DyzAbnuc.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/_astro/_id_.w1YKO1jA.css"></head><body><a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header shell"><a class="wordmark" href="/"><img src="/favicon.svg" width="36" height="36" alt=""><span>Agentic Bible<span class="wordmark-sub">A living reference</span></span></a><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/" aria-current="page">Library</a><a href="/topics/">Topics</a><a href="/about/">About</a><a href="/history/">History</a></nav><a class="header-search" href="/search/" aria-label="Search the library"><svg width="17" height="17" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="8.5" cy="8.5" r="5.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"></circle><path d="m13 13 4 4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"></path></svg><span>Search</span><kbd aria-hidden="true">/</kbd></a></header><div class="preview-strip" role="region" aria-label="Edition status"><div class="shell"><span class="status-dot"></span> Preview edition <span class="strip-detail">Sourced working drafts. Editorial review pending.</span></div></div><main id="main" class="shell" tabindex="-1"><div class="article-breadcrumb" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="/" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Library</a><span data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>/</span><a href="/topics/capabilities/" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Capabilities</a></div><div class="reading-layout" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><article class="article" data-pagefind-body data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><header id="overview" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><p class="eyebrow" data-pagefind-meta="topic" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Capabilities / Reference entry 05</p><h1 data-pagefind-meta="title" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Agent memory</h1><p class="article-deck" data-pagefind-meta="description" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>How an agent stores and retrieves information across steps or sessions, and why persistence, relevance, and truth are separate questions.</p><div class="article-status" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><span class="draft-pill" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><span class="status-dot" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></span> Working draft</span><span data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Human editorial certification pending</span></div><nav class="article-actions" aria-label="Article actions" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="#sources" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Sources (3)</a><a href="#revision" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Revision history</a><a href="#share" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Share or cite</a><a href="/contribute/?article=agent-memory" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Contribution options</a></nav></header><aside class="contents" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><details data-article-contents data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><summary data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>On this page</summary><nav aria-label="On this page" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="#overview" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Overview</a><a href="#what-is-actually-being-retained" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>What is actually being retained?</a><a href="#reading-and-writing-are-separate-decisions" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Reading and writing are separate decisions</a><a href="#persistence-is-not-verification" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Persistence is not verification</a><a href="#evaluate-remembering-and-forgetting" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Evaluate remembering and forgetting</a><a href="#sources" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Sources & context</a><a href="#revision" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Revision record</a></nav></details><div class="contents-bottom" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><span class="eyebrow" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Part of the collection</span><a href="/topics/capabilities/" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Capabilities <svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></a></div></aside><div class="prose" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><p><strong>Agent memory</strong> refers here to information an <a href="/articles/ai-agent/">agent</a> retains and can use in later decisions. The term covers several mechanisms. LangChain’s documentation distinguishes conversation or thread-scoped state from information shared across sessions in a longer-lived store. Its use of “short-term” and “long-term” describes recall scope; even thread state may be persisted in a database. These labels do not by themselves specify a retention period. <a href="#source-langgraph-memory">3</a></p>
<h2 id="what-is-actually-being-retained">What is actually being retained?</h2>
<p>A useful distinction is between text currently supplied to a model and records held outside that context. MemGPT makes this separation explicit. Its architecture uses function calls to move information between a fixed context and external archival or conversation storage. Keeping more information externally does not put all of it into one inference: the system must retrieve and move the relevant material into context. The paper evaluates document analysis and multi-session conversation, not unrestricted perfect recall. <a href="#source-memgpt">2</a></p>
<p>Another research example is Generative Agents. In its simulated town, agents record observations, retrieve memories using recency, importance, and relevance, and form higher-level reflections to inform behavior. A reflection is generated interpretation of stored experience. The study concerns believable simulated behavior, so it would be a mistake to treat a retrieved reflection as independently verified knowledge or as evidence of human-like experience. <a href="#source-generative-agents">1</a></p>
<p>These examples concern information managed around model calls. A stored note and a change to a model’s learned parameters are different mechanisms. Describing an application as remembering a preference does not establish that its underlying model has been retrained. The storage and retrieval path needs to be specified before making that claim.</p>
<h2 id="reading-and-writing-are-separate-decisions">Reading and writing are separate decisions</h2>
<p>LangChain describes memory updates during a request or in background work. Immediate updates can be available to the next interaction but add work to the current response; background updates introduce a question about when other interactions see the new information. This is implementation guidance, not evidence that either timing policy is best for every application. <a href="#source-langgraph-memory">3</a></p>
<p>Consider an original example: a research assistant records that an article’s protocol reference needs checking. A useful record could include the article, the source revision, why it was questioned, and whether the issue remains open. A later run could retrieve that record before repeating the investigation. Nothing in this example requires the assistant to store a user’s unrelated conversation history.</p>
<p>Suppose a subsequent check resolves the issue. Simply appending “resolved” while leaving the earlier warning equally eligible for retrieval would create ambiguity. In this proposed design, the new record would identify the earlier record it supersedes, retain the evidence for the correction, and make the current status visible. This is a design illustration rather than a claim that this encyclopedia already has such memory.</p>
<h2 id="persistence-is-not-verification">Persistence is not verification</h2>
<p>The example also separates what happened from what a model inferred. “A request failed” is a different record from “the document no longer exists.” A system should not silently promote the second statement from the first. This entry’s proposed record format would label observations and interpretations separately so future work could revisit the inference.</p>
<p><a href="/articles/retrieval-augmented-generation/">Retrieval-augmented generation</a> may supply records to a response, but selecting a relevant record does not resolve whether it is current, accurate, or appropriate to disclose. An external instruction stored as a note could also reappear later; the <a href="/articles/prompt-injection/">prompt-injection</a> entry explains why source text needs a different authority from application instructions.</p>
<h2 id="evaluate-remembering-and-forgetting">Evaluate remembering and forgetting</h2>
<p>For the research-assistant example, a useful <a href="/articles/agent-evaluation/">evaluation</a> would test whether a later run retrieves the correction, avoids reviving the obsolete warning, and respects the intended scope of the record. A separate test would remove a record and check both the store and later answers. An answer that happens to omit it would not prove deletion.</p>
<p>Retention, access, correction, and deletion are explicit design decisions in this example. Neither a memory label nor a convincing recollection demonstrates that those decisions have been implemented. <a href="/articles/agent-orchestration/">Orchestration</a> determines when records flow between steps; memory determines what can survive those steps. Both need evidence about their actual behavior.</p>
</div><p class="article-feedback" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="/contribute/?article=agent-memory" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Suggest a correction or primary source</a> · Public intake remains on hold; the local synthetic form covers only the original three entries.</p><section class="sources" id="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><p class="eyebrow" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Follow the evidence</p><h2 id="sources-title" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Sources & context</h2><p data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Primary sources checked on <time datetime="2026-10-16" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>16 October 2026</time>. A source check is not independent editorial approval.</p><ol data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><li id="source-generative-agents" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03442v2" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior <span aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></span></a><span class="source-publisher" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Park et al., arXiv</span><p data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Version 2, 6 August 2023; section 4 describes memory, retrieval, reflection, and planning in a simulated town. Believability in that setting is not factual reliability or evidence of consciousness.</p></li><li id="source-memgpt" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08560v2" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>MemGPT: Towards LLMs as Operating Systems <span aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></span></a><span class="source-publisher" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Packer et al., arXiv</span><p data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Version 2, 12 February 2024; section 2 describes movement between a fixed model context and external stores. The operating-system comparison is an architectural analogy.</p></li><li id="source-langgraph-memory" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/concepts/memory" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Memory overview <span aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></span></a><span class="source-publisher" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>LangChain</span><p data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Living documentation checked 16 October 2026; thread-scoped checkpoints, cross-thread stores, and memory-writing tradeoffs describe this implementation.</p></li></ol></section><section class="revision" id="revision" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><h2 data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Revision record</h2><dl data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Authorship</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>AI-assisted project draft</dd></div><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Source check</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><time datetime="2026-10-16" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>16 October 2026</time>. This records source consultation, not editorial certification.</dd></div><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Human editorial review</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Pending. No human editorial certification is recorded.</dd></div><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Latest source change</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><time datetime="2026-10-16" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>16 October 2026</time> · Add five sourced encyclopedia entries and expand bounded discovery · <code data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>398bacc7</code></dd></div><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Revision receipt</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>No separate publication receipt is recorded for this working draft.</dd></div></dl><p class="review-scope" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>The twenty entries received deep reading across external and internal review. Claim verification was sampled; this does not certify every factual claim.</p><a class="text-link" data-source-commit="8a38252384c6fe6024a75b462ad95294a131100c" href="https://github.com/Leshey-Digital/agentic-bible/commits/8a38252384c6fe6024a75b462ad95294a131100c/src/content/articles/agent-memory.md" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>View file history on GitHub <svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></a></section><section class="share-tools" id="share" aria-labelledby="share-title" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><p class="eyebrow" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Keep a useful reference</p><h2 id="share-title" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Share or cite this entry</h2><p id="article-citation" tabindex="0" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>“Agent memory.” Agentic Bible. https://agenticbible.si/articles/agent-memory/</p><div class="share-buttons" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><button type="button" data-copy-link hidden data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Copy article link</button><button type="button" data-copy-citation hidden data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Copy citation</button></div><p class="share-status" data-share-status role="status" aria-live="polite" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>The citation and link above remain available to copy manually if clipboard access is unavailable.</p></sect